        "expect": "reject"
      }
    ],
    "library": {
      "author": {
        "login": "thethinkmachine"
      },
      "license": "CC-BY-4.0",
      "tags": [
        "not-context-free",
        "queue",
        "counting"
      ],
      "difficulty": "intermediate",
      "readme": "A stack forgets as it checks: once the b's have popped the a's, there is nothing left to compare the c's with. A queue does not have to forget. Every **b** dequeues an A from the front and enqueues a B at the back, so after the b's the queue holds exactly as many B's as there were b's — provided every A was matched, which is checked because a b arriving when a B is at the front has no move.\n\nQueue automata are as powerful as Turing machines (a queue can be rotated to reach any cell), so this is far from the limit of what they can do. It is only the smallest thing a stack cannot."
    }
  }
}
